[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Grooming no longer moves a node between files

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-GROOMED-NODES-STAY-IN-THE-BACKLOG-FILE
TITLE: Grooming no longer moves a node between files
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Abandoned. MECH-ONE-NODE-PER-FILE replaced the work this node scheduled.

The problem was that the backlog protocol defined grooming as moving a node into
a numbered document in the same plan, and the 2026-08-27 grooming pass raised
DEPTH on eighteen nodes and moved none of them, leaving a backlog document full
of interface-settled nodes whose name no longer described it. The work was
deciding which numbered document each of the eighteen belonged to, and moving
them.

The carve dissolved it rather than doing it. Every node is its own file now, so
there is no backlog document to be misnamed, no numbered document to move into,
and no file-level signal to lose: opening a file tells you about exactly one
node. What replaced the file-level signal is an edge -- an item carries
Backlogged_In to its plan's register, per DEC-BACKLOG-IS-A-REGISTER-NODE -- so
the backlog is readable by traversal rather than by opening a directory.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Kept rather than deleted, per DEC-NO-DELETES-YET, and rewritten in place rather
than superseded, which DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE permits on a MECHANISM and which is
the only path available anyway: Superseded_By exists on DECISION alone.

The original reason for logging instead of acting still reads correctly and is
worth preserving, because it is why the eighteen nodes were still sitting in one
file when the carve found them. Moving eighteen nodes between files in the same
pass that rewrote their contents would have made the diff unreviewable, on a
branch whose whole review model is the carve-up into pull requests. The carve
avoided that by moving all 161 nodes and rewriting none of them.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The UID preserves the retired shape and the TITLE no longer does. Renaming
either was not an option: the filename tracks the UID, the UID is hand-chosen
and never changes, and that invariant is exactly what let the carve move every
node without breaking a citation.

Logged against MECH-SLICE-CANNOT-BE-SUPERSEDED as an instance of a non-DECISION
node being superseded in prose. That makes two events for that node to weigh,
not one.

The rough split by subject recorded at the time of logging -- readiness and
predicate nodes with the tooling document, plan-refs and lifecycle nodes with
the lifecycle document, CLI, guard and signing nodes with the executable-rules
document -- has no destinations left to name. It is kept only as evidence that
the grouping was a judgement per node, which is part of why a flat directory
plus edges was the cheaper answer.
<<<
RELATIONS:
